From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Casota <dcasota@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] installer: ISO installs follow the boot firmware and release
 DHCP leases

Two defects of an install from the ISO, fixed together because the
second depends on the first.

1. Bootmode and live. Since 1e80f1802 _add_defaults() sets bootmode to
   'efi' when the configuration names none, the curses UI never names
   one, and nothing reads the firmware that booted the media: an
   interactive install on a legacy-BIOS VM leaves a GPT with only the ESP
   and the root partition, no boot code in sector 0 and no
   /boot/grub2/i386-pc, so the firmware finds no bootable disk. The same
   install runs with live=False, because isoInstaller stamps live only on
   a non-empty kickstart configuration: the CD is not ejected, efibootmgr
   is not run and /etc/machine-id stays uninitialized. isoInstaller now
   hands its media defaults to Installer.configure(), which applies them
   with setdefault() once the configuration exists, from a kickstart or
   from the UI: live=True, and bootmode 'efi' when /sys/firmware/efi
   exists or the machine is aarch64, 'dualboot' otherwise. Values a
   kickstart names win; image builds call configure() without defaults
   and are unchanged.

2. DHCP leases. The installer environment holds DHCPv4 leases that it
   never releases (its .network sets SendRelease=no); the installed system
   then asks the same server with a new client identity. VMware's
   vmnetdhcp offers it the address the installer still holds on that MAC,
   then refuses the REQUEST with a DHCPNAK that carries no Server
   Identifier, which systemd-networkd ignores ("NAK from unexpected
   server"); the client retries until the installer's lease expires, 30
   minutes, and systemd-networkd-wait-online fails meanwhile. Before the
   manifest is written, a live install now stops the DHCP client of every
   link that holds a DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 address: dhcprelease.release_leases()
   writes a runtime override per link (DHCP=no, SendRelease=yes) under
   /run/systemd/network, reloads networkd once and waits until every link
   reports the override applied and no DHCP address left.

   Link state comes from `networkctl --json=short list` (Name, NetworkFile,
   Addresses[].ConfigSource), the public description networkd gives in
   systemd 253 and 257 alike, not from the private files under
   /run/systemd/netif. All links share one reload and one deadline, so
   the worst case does not grow with the number of NICs: at most three
   setup commands of COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SEC (10 s) and RELEASE_TIMEOUT_SEC
   (10 s) of polling every POLL_INTERVAL_SEC (0.2 s), 40 s in all, every
   poll capped by the time left. It runs only when networkd is active (so
   it never starts it), skips link names that are globs, and never
   raises: a failure must not fail an otherwise good install. The outcome
   per link (released, timeout, skipped) with any error and the duration
   goes to the installer log and to dhcp_release in the manifest, of
   which the installer writes a copy to /var/log/poi on the installed
   system, the one record that survives the reboot. It is gated on
   live, the flag the installer already uses for changes to its own
   environment (networkd restart, CD eject), and part 1 is what makes
   live true for interactive installs.

A release alone does not make the installed system's first request
succeed. Every installer booted from one ISO shares the machine-id that
the initrd builder writes at build time, so they share one DUID and, all
naming the NIC enp0s17, one DHCP client identifier; vmnetdhcp keys
leases on it, hands two installers the same address and matches one
installer's DHCPRELEASE to another installer's lease. Even with unique
identifiers it ignores some releases: on the same ISO the release freed
the lease on some MACs and was dropped on others (host and guest
captures). The installed system then presents a different identifier
than the client that still holds the address, and runs into the NAK
loop above.

So the installer environment and the installed system use the same
DHCPv4 client identifier, the MAC address (ClientIdentifier=mac): the
installed system's first REQUEST comes from the client that holds the
lease and is acknowledged whether or not the release was honoured.
networkmanager writes ClientIdentifier= for every interface with DHCPv4,
from the netplan key "dhcp-identifier" ("mac" by default, "duid" for
systemd's default), and the initrd builder adds ClientIdentifier=mac
with SendRelease=yes as a drop-in for every .network file of the
installer environment. The DHCPv6 DUID of the installer environment is
derived from the link-layer address (networkd.conf.d), unique per NIC;
the machine-id and its first-boot semantics stay as they are.

Whether stopping a DHCPv4 client sends DHCPRELEASE is decided in two
different places depending on the systemd version. systemd 257.13 reads
SendRelease= of the .network attached when the client stops, so the
override's SendRelease=yes takes effect. Since systemd v260 (upstream
68c70042365d, backported to Photon's 257.13) the client records
SendRelease= when it is configured, and stopping the client created at
boot under the installer's SendRelease=no sends nothing; the drop-in
creates that client with releases enabled, so the override releases it
under either behaviour. Installed systems keep SendRelease= as shipped.

tests/test_dhcprelease.py covers the release logic with networkctl and
systemctl faked: networkd inactive, networkctl missing, no DHCP links,
release after the override applies, one reload and one deadline for
several links, a vanished link, glob names, a failing command,
unexpected JSON, and a timeout on every command; and the initrd: it keeps
networkctl, carries the link-layer DHCPv6 DUID configuration, and sets
ClientIdentifier=mac and SendRelease= for every .network file it has.
tests/test_networkmanager.py covers the client identifier networkmanager
writes: "mac" by default, "duid" on request, an unknown value refused,
none for static and DHCPv6-only interfaces, legacy and VLAN DHCP. Run
with: pytest -x tests/test_dhcprelease.py tests/test_networkmanager.py

The release drives networkd through networkctl, and the installer's
initrd builder deleted /usr/bin/networkctl to save space, so on a real ISO
the release failed with FileNotFoundError before it did anything. Keep
networkctl in the initrd. Should it be missing all the same, the release
records "networkctl is not installed" instead of an unexplained OSError.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
